  "meta": {
    "title": "2026-06-09-post-mortem-visuals · candidate-008 (avatar mask fix — no band-split)"
  },
  "agent": {
    "note": "Iter 8 — fixing the broken iter7 layout you caught. Root cause: placeRects band-splits rects in the same y-row when they have different heights — my 40-tall avatar + 100-tall body shared a row, so body decomposed into a 40-tall top band (alongside avatar) + 60-tall bottom band. spec-index → stableKey mapping lined up wrong for the split frames, so the avatar circle mask landed on a 600×40 body-top frame (stretched into the orange ellipse you saw) and labels misaligned. Fix: avatar rect is now 40×100 (matches body height — no band-splitting). The 40px circle mask lives in the TOP of the 40×100 bounds (mask path coords use the full 40×100 box, so the engine scales 1:1 and the circle stays a circle). End state: 9 input rects → 9 output frames; masks present on exactly the 4 avatar frames; nothing leaks onto body rects.",
    "question": "Layout should now be evaluable. Open with Rects + Masks + Label colors on. You should see: orange human bubbles (right) with darker-orange circle avatars in top-right; dark agent bubbles (left) with pale-steel circle avatars in top-left; full-width gray system separator; bg #14151b. If still off — what specifically? Also: should we extend placeRects (or add a higher-level builder) to take input→output stableKey mapping AS data, so labels/masks bind to input rects rather than output frame index? That would prevent this whole class of mistake.",
    "context": {
      "category": {
        "kind": "known",
        "value": "edge-case-handling"
      },
      "intent": {
        "action": "refine",
        "summary": "fix band-split mask leak; same-Y rect heights per row"
      },
      "relation": {
        "kind": "refines",
        "target": "007"
      },
      "layoutIntent": "free"
    },
    "response": {
      "body": "the human and agent avatars are not squares. reject layout",
      "from": "human",
      "at": "2026-06-10T03:58:25.908Z"
    }
  },
  "size": {
    "width": 768,
    "height": 1080
  },
